sugar equilibration; subsequent dehydration can remove some aldotetrose.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Enediol-mediated carbonyl migration converts a ketotetrose into aldotetrose stereoisomers.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is an established carbohydrate-isomerization class, not a claim of selective erythrose synthesis. Both erythrose-like and threose-like configurations are represented by the stereochemically unspecified aldotetrose node. The equilibrium and degradation rates must be measured for any proposed natural setting.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldotetrose dehydration to 3-deoxytetrosone\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Warm concentrating pool; approximately pH 4-7 after leaving the alkaline sugar-forming compartment.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Enolization, dehydration, and carbonyl rearrangement generate a 3-deoxyosone connectivity, OHC-CO-CH2-CH2OH.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Deoxyosone formation is grounded in reducing-sugar degradation chemistry, but useful accumulation of this C4 product in a prebiotic pool is speculative. Fragmentation, alternative dehydration products, and browning reactions compete. Transfer away from the alkaline aldol compartment is chemically motivated by the different reaction requirements.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Debus-type formation of 4-(2-hydroxyethyl)imidazole\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated ammoniacal pool, approximately pH 7.5-9.5; partial evaporation followed by rehydration.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The adjacent carbonyl groups of 3-deoxytetrosone condense with ammonia and formaldehyde, followed by ring closure and dehydration to a substituted imidazole. The hydroxyethyl substituent is retained at ring carbon 4 or its tautomerically equivalent position.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The reaction family is established by Debus-type imidazole synthesis (Debus, 1858), but this exact prebiotic substrate combination and its selectivity are unvalidated. The dicarbonyl supplies two ring carbons and formaldehyde supplies the third, giving the correct side-chain length. Ammonia-carbonyl oligomers and alternative heterocycles may dominate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothetical anoxic photodehydrogenation to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"CdS semiconductor surface, represented geologically by greenockite\",\n        \"NiS hydrogen-evolution cocatalyst, represented geologically by millerite\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm with low hydrogen partial pressure\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Illuminated CdS/NiS interface\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic irradiated mineral interface; approximately pH 7-9; physically associated sulfide phases; rapid aldehyde trapping; free sulfide must not consume all photogenerated holes.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photogenerated holes oxidize the primary alcohol to an aldehyde, while conduction-band electrons support proton reduction and hydrogen evolution. Light supplies the energy for acceptorless dehydrogenation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the principal speculative redox bottleneck, not an experimentally established prebiotic histidine step. Semiconductor alcohol oxidation coupled to hydrogen evolution motivates the chemistry, but performance of engineered photocatalysts cannot be assumed for natural grains. Mineral rarity, photocorrosion, imidazole oxidation, and poor aldehyde selectivity could invalidate this branch. Molecular oxygen is neither an input nor an implicit oxidant.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Imidazole-4-acetaldehyde imine formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-rich concentrated solution, approximately pH 8-9.5; partial drying and immediate cyanide trapping.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to the aldehyde, and dehydration of the carbinolamine establishes an imine/iminium equilibrium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is standard entry chemistry for a Strecker synthesis (Strecker, 1850). The imine need not accumulate or be isolated because cyanide can trap a small equilibrium population. Its abundance, hydrolysis, and competing reactions with this imidazole-containing aldehyde remain substrate-specific uncertainties.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide addition to the histidine-precursor imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-325 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 8-9.5; retained cyanide inventory; limited irradiation or mineral shading during accumulation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer, forming the alpha-aminonitrile without stereocontrol.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The bond-forming pattern is the classical Strecker reaction (Strecker, 1850). Crucially, the aldehyde already has an imidazolylmethyl substituent; using imidazole-4-carbaldehyde instead would give the wrong amino-acid skeleton. Prebiotic availability of the required aldehyde, rather than this generic reaction class, is the major unresolved issue.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-first capture of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 7.5-9; cyanide arrives before a large ammonia pulse; cool, partly shaded water limits photodegradation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible cyanide addition to the aldehyde followed by proton transfer forms its cyanohydrin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanohydrin formation is established aldehyde chemistry and supplies an operational alternative when cyanide precedes ammonia. Formation or persistence of this specific cyanohydrin in a natural mixture is not established. It is a potential reversible carbonyl reservoir, not evidence for a separate fundamental amino-acid synthesis mechanism.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-driven conversion of the cyanohydrin reservoir to aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-rich solution, approximately pH 8-9.5; cyanide retained during equilibration.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The net conversion proceeds through reversible cyanohydrin dissociation, ammonia capture of the aldehyde, and cyanide trapping of imine/iminium species. Direct substitution of an unactivated hydroxyl group is not assumed.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a chemically defensible cyanohydrin-fed Strecker operation, but substrate-specific prebiotic conversion is unvalidated. It shares transient species with rxn_011 and rxn_012 and therefore represents a different feed-order pathway, not an independent elementary mechanism. Ammonia and cyanide loss can prevent useful conversion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Histidine aminonitrile hydration to histidinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions in acidic water\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-410 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-100 bar, maintaining liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 2-4 in cooled acidic hydrothermal pore water; transport from surface synthesis is required; avoid the hottest vent fluids.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Protonation of the nitrile promotes water addition and tautomerization to the primary amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrile hydration is established chemistry, including the hydrolytic completion of Strecker syntheses. The proposed temperature-acidity window is a candidate geochemical setting, not a measured optimum for this substrate. Aminonitrile decomposition and retro-Strecker chemistry may outcompete hydration, and transport without excessive dilution is unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Histidinamide hydrolysis to histidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions in acidic water\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"390-430 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-100 bar, maintaining liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 2-4; extended but finite residence followed by cooling; ammonia is mainly ammonium in situ.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed addition of water to the amide carbonyl and carbon-nitrogen bond cleavage yield the amino acid and ammonia equivalents.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amide hydrolysis completes the conventional Strecker carbon skeleton. Whether hydrolysis is sufficiently rapid under these comparatively dilute natural acidic conditions is an important kinetic gap. Histidine decomposition and racemization constrain residence time; a viable process must cool the product before destructive reactions dominate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs-type conversion of aminonitrile to histidine hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ammonium carbonate/bicarbonate buffer\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"325-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ammonium carbonate/bicarbonate acid-base medium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 8-9.5; concentrated NH3/CO2-bearing pool; sustained inorganic-carbon supply.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aminonitrile carbamation, cyclization through imino-oxazolidinone-type species, and rearrangement produce a hydantoin. The net atom balance is aminonitrile plus carbon dioxide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This invokes the established Bucherer-Bergs reaction family (Bucherer and Steiner, 1934). It offers a chemically distinct downstream reservoir rather than another carbon-skeleton construction. Conversion of this particular imidazole-bearing aminonitrile under natural pool conditions is unvalidated; inorganic-carbon speciation and competing imidazole interactions require testing.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydantoin ring opening to N-carbamoylhistidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide in alkaline hydrothermal water\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"390-430 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-100 bar, maintaining liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous hydroxide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 10-11 in a cooled alkaline circulation zone; long residence may be required.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide-assisted attack on the hydantoin carbonyl corresponding to the original nitrile carbon opens the ring to the N-carbamoyl amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin hydrolysis is established preparative chemistry, but commonly requires more forcing alkalinity than natural alkaline vents provide. Consequently, useful conversion at the stated pH is speculative and is a major bottleneck of these pathways. Alternative ring cleavage, incomplete conversion, and product destruction must be quantified rather than assumed negligible.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"N-Carbamoylhistidine hydrolysis and decarbamoylation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions in acidic water\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"390-430 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-100 bar, maintaining liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 2-4 after transport to a separate acidic circulation zone; subsequent cooling is necessary.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic cleavage of the N-carbamoyl group releases histidine; the detached carbamoyl carbon and nitrogen appear ultimately as carbon dioxide and ammonia equivalents.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nonenzymatic decarbamoylation is chemically possible, but a productive rate for this substrate under the stated natural conditions is not established. Recyclization to hydantoin and amino-acid degradation are competing processes. The alkaline-to-acidic transfer is required by this proposed hydrolysis sequence and is an explicit geochemical vulnerability, not a decorative environmental connection.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolaldehyde dimer route with imine-first Strecker hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Aldotetrose formation and dehydration supply the dicarbonyl; imidazole construction and hypothetical photodehydrogenation precede imine-first aminonitrile formation and direct hydrolysis. Candidate route, not experimentally demonstrated end to end.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolaldehyde dimer route with cyanohydrin-first Strecker hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The same aldotetrose-derived aldehyde is initially captured as a cyanohydrin before ammonia-driven equilibration to aminonitrile and direct hydrolysis. This differs from P1 in operational entry order, not the underlying Strecker mechanism.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolaldehyde dimer route with imine-first hydantoin processing\",\n      \"description\": \"The aldotetrose branch supplies the aldehyde, imine-first Strecker chemistry supplies aminonitrile, and carbon dioxide capture diverts it through hydantoin and N-carbamoylhistidine. Both photodehydrogenation and geochemically mild hydantoin hydrolysis remain unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolaldehyde dimer route with cyanohydrin-first hydantoin processing\",\n      \"description\": \"An aldotetrose-derived aldehyde passes through a cyanohydrin reservoir, aminonitrile, hydantoin, and N-carbamoylhistidine. This is a feed-order variant of P3 with the same unresolved precursor and hydrolysis bottlenecks.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Methylglyoxal chain-extension route with imine-first Strecker hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyceraldehyde dehydration and speculative methylglyoxal hydroxymethylation replace aldotetrose dehydration. The resulting common imidazole aldehyde undergoes imine-first Strecker chemistry and direct aminonitrile hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Methylglyoxal chain-extension route with cyanohydrin-first Strecker hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The methylglyoxal branch supplies the imidazole aldehyde, which is cyanated before ammonia capture. Direct aminonitrile hydrolysis then gives racemic histidine, conditional on all unresolved precursor steps.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Methylglyoxal chain-extension route with imine-first hydantoin processing\",\n      \"description\": \"Methylglyoxal hydroxymethylation supplies the C4 precursor; imine-first Strecker chemistry is followed by hydantoin formation, ring opening, and decarbamoylation. This combines two substantial uncertainties: C4 precursor accumulation and mild hydantoin processing.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Methylglyoxal chain-extension route with cyanohydrin-first hydantoin processing\",\n      \"description\": \"The methylglyoxal branch feeds a cyanohydrin-first Strecker operation, followed by carbon dioxide incorporation into hydantoin and subsequent hydrolysis. Cyanohydrin staging is operationally distinct but not mechanistically independent of imine-first entry.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Ketotetrose chain-extension route with direct Strecker hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyceraldehyde isomerizes to dihydroxyacetone, which adds formaldehyde to give erythrulose. Isomerization and dehydration feed the common imidazole-building sequence, followed by imine-first aminonitrile formation and direct hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Ketotetrose chain-extension route with hydantoin processing\",\n      \"description\": \"The dihydroxyacetone-erythrulose branch supplies aldotetrose and the common imidazole precursor. Imine-first Strecker chemistry then feeds hydantoin formation and hydrolysis. This longer candidate route is chemically connected but offers no demonstrated yield advantage.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_012\",\n    \"mol_015\",\n    \"mol_018\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_012\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_015\", \"rxn_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_016\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Histidine", "overall_strategy": "This is a connected, explicitly hypothetical network for racemic histidine, not a demonstrated end-to-end prebiotic synthesis. Ten graph-distinct operational pathways combine three routes to a four-carbon dicarbonyl precursor with imine-first or cyanohydrin-first Strecker chemistry and either direct nitrile hydrolysis or hydantoin-mediated hydrolysis. These combinations are not ten independent mechanisms; cyanohydrin-first and imine-first routes share underlying equilibria. The principal unresolved steps are substituted-imidazole formation, anoxic oxidation to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde, and sufficiently mild final hydrolysis. Simple feedstocks are assumed locally available; their concentrations and environmental delivery remain boundary conditions. Reactions represent selected net transformations, not exhaustive product distributions. Neutral reference structures and unspecified stereochemistry are used; aqueous protonation states, hydrates, and stereoisomers are not separately enumerated. No molecular oxygen, enzymes, or enantioselection are assumed. Hydrogen is a terminal coproduct assigned the schema's intermediate role because a coproduct role is unavailable.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "Methanal", "common_name": "Formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "2-Hydroxyethanal", "common_name": "Glycolaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Oxidane", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "Azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "Carbon dioxide", "common_name": "Carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C4H8O4", "iupac_name": "2,3,4-Trihydroxybutanal", "common_name": "Open-chain aldotetrose, stereochemistry unspecified", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C3H6O3", "iupac_name": "2,3-Dihydroxypropanal", "common_name": "Glyceraldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C3H4O2", "iupac_name": "2-Oxopropanal", "common_name": "Methylglyoxal", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C3H6O3", "iupac_name": "1,3-Dihydroxypropan-2-one", "common_name": "Dihydroxyacetone", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C4H8O4", "iupac_name": "1,3,4-Trihydroxybutan-2-one", "common_name": "Erythrulose", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C4H6O3", "iupac_name": "4-Hydroxy-2-oxobutanal", "common_name": "3-Deoxytetrosone", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C5H8N2O", "iupac_name": "2-(1H-Imidazol-4-yl)ethan-1-ol", "common_name": "4-(2-Hydroxyethyl)imidazole", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "Dihydrogen", "common_name": "Hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H6N2O", "iupac_name": "2-(1H-Imidazol-4-yl)ethanal", "common_name": "Imidazole-4-acetaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C5H7N3", "iupac_name": "2-(1H-Imidazol-4-yl)ethanimine", "common_name": "Imidazole-4-acetaldehyde imine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C6H7N3O", "iupac_name": "2-Hydroxy-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanenitrile", "common_name": "Imidazole-4-acetaldehyde cyanohydrin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C6H8N4", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanenitrile", "common_name": "Histidine aminonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "C6H10N4O", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanamide", "common_name": "Histidinamide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "C7H8N4O2", "iupac_name": "5-[(1H-Imidazol-4-yl)methyl]imidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "Histidine hydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "C7H10N4O3", "iupac_name": "2-(Carbamoylamino)-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanoic acid", "common_name": "N-Carbamoylhistidine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "C6H9N3O2", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanoic acid", "common_name": "DL-Histidine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C6H9N3O2/c7-5(6(10)11)1-4-2-8-3-9-4/h2-3,5H,1,7H2,(H,8,9)(H,10,11)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Glycolaldehyde self-aldol addition to an aldotetrose", "inputs": ["mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved calcium ions and hydroxide in alkaline water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "310-340 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Ca2+/OH- aldol-catalytic medium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Transient alkaline pool, approximately pH 10-11; evaporation concentrates glycolaldehyde; avoid prolonged heating."}, "mechanism": "An enediolate derived from one glycolaldehyde attacks the carbonyl of another, producing the linear aldotetrose skeleton. Two glycolaldehyde equivalents are implied without repeating the molecule ID.", "reasoning": "This is a chemically established formose-type carbon-carbon bond-forming reaction. Calcium is justified by its coordination-assisted aldol chemistry, not by a generic mineral effect. Erythrose and threose configurations, further aldol products, and degradation products are expected; the node records the selected stereochemically unresolved connectivity, not a pure product."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Glycolaldehyde-formaldehyde aldol addition", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved calcium ions and hydroxide in alkaline water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-335 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Ca2+/OH- aldol-catalytic medium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 9.5-11; intermittent formaldehyde supply; short residence before extensive sugar scrambling."}, "mechanism": "Glycolaldehyde enolization followed by addition to formaldehyde extends the carbon skeleton to glyceraldehyde.", "reasoning": "This is a standard formose-network transformation using simple carbonyl feedstocks. It does not require selective oxidation or biological catalysis. Competition with glycolaldehyde self-addition and subsequent sugar reactions prevents assuming a high isolated glyceraldehyde yield."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Glyceraldehyde dehydration to methylglyoxal", "inputs": ["mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "335-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Warm concentrated pool; approximately pH 6-9; repeated concentration and dilution."}, "mechanism": "Enolization and dehydration convert the triose into the alpha-dicarbonyl methylglyoxal without requiring an external oxidant.", "reasoning": "Methylglyoxal formation is a recognized nonenzymatic triose-degradation channel. The net conversion is dehydration, not oxygen-dependent oxidation. Its usefulness here depends on trapping methylglyoxal before polymerization, disproportionation, or other degradation reactions dominate."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Methylglyoxal hydroxymethylation to 3-deoxytetrosone", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide in mildly alkaline water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Aqueous hydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 8.5-10; limited formaldehyde exposure followed by prompt consumption of the dicarbonyl product."}, "mechanism": "Enolization at methylglyoxal's methyl group enables aldol addition to formaldehyde, giving OHC-CO-CH2-CH2OH.", "reasoning": "This substrate-specific prebiotic branch is speculative but follows conventional aldol connectivity and is atom-balanced. Hydration, rearrangement to lactate-related products, repeated hydroxymethylation, and oligomerization compete. No selective accumulation of this particular dicarbonyl under the stated conditions is claimed."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Glyceraldehyde-dihydroxyacetone isomerization", "inputs": ["mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide in mildly alkaline water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-335 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Aqueous hydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 8-10; reversible equilibration; carbonyl degradation competes."}, "mechanism": "A shared enediol or enediolate transfers the carbonyl position from the terminal aldehyde to the central ketone.", "reasoning": "Base-catalyzed aldose-ketose interconversion is established carbohydrate chemistry. Only the forward direction used by the listed pathways is drawn. This route is included because dihydroxyacetone can undergo linear chain extension; direct hydroxymethylation of glyceraldehyde would not necessarily yield the desired linear tetrose."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Dihydroxyacetone-formaldehyde aldol addition", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved calcium ions and hydroxide in alkaline water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-335 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Ca2+/OH- aldol-catalytic medium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 9-10.5; dilute formaldehyde pulses into a concentrated triose solution."}, "mechanism": "A terminal dihydroxyacetone enediolate attacks formaldehyde, forming the linear ketotetrose erythrulose.", "reasoning": "The carbon connectivity follows established formose-type aldol chemistry. The symmetric triose permits formation of the required linear four-carbon skeleton without a special regioselective catalyst. Product stereochemistry is uncontrolled, and higher-sugar formation remains a substantial competing sink."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Erythrulose-aldotetrose isomerization", "inputs": ["mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide in mildly alkaline water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Aqueous hydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 8-10; reversible sugar equilibration; subsequent dehydration can remove some aldotetrose."}, "mechanism": "Enediol-mediated carbonyl migration converts a ketotetrose into aldotetrose stereoisomers.", "reasoning": "This is an established carbohydrate-isomerization class, not a claim of selective erythrose synthesis. Both erythrose-like and threose-like configurations are represented by the stereochemically unspecified aldotetrose node. The equilibrium and degradation rates must be measured for any proposed natural setting."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Aldotetrose dehydration to 3-deoxytetrosone", "inputs": ["mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Warm concentrating pool; approximately pH 4-7 after leaving the alkaline sugar-forming compartment."}, "mechanism": "Enolization, dehydration, and carbonyl rearrangement generate a 3-deoxyosone connectivity, OHC-CO-CH2-CH2OH.", "reasoning": "Deoxyosone formation is grounded in reducing-sugar degradation chemistry, but useful accumulation of this C4 product in a prebiotic pool is speculative. Fragmentation, alternative dehydration products, and browning reactions compete. Transfer away from the alkaline aldol compartment is chemically motivated by the different reaction requirements."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Debus-type formation of 4-(2-hydroxyethyl)imidazole", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-365 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated ammoniacal pool, approximately pH 7.5-9.5; partial evaporation followed by rehydration."}, "mechanism": "The adjacent carbonyl groups of 3-deoxytetrosone condense with ammonia and formaldehyde, followed by ring closure and dehydration to a substituted imidazole. The hydroxyethyl substituent is retained at ring carbon 4 or its tautomerically equivalent position.", "reasoning": "The reaction family is established by Debus-type imidazole synthesis (Debus, 1858), but this exact prebiotic substrate combination and its selectivity are unvalidated. The dicarbonyl supplies two ring carbons and formaldehyde supplies the third, giving the correct side-chain length. Ammonia-carbonyl oligomers and alternative heterocycles may dominate."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Hypothetical anoxic photodehydrogenation to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_013"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["CdS semiconductor surface, represented geologically by greenockite", "NiS hydrogen-evolution cocatalyst, represented geologically by millerite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm with low hydrogen partial pressure", "catalyst": "Illuminated CdS/NiS interface", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic irradiated mineral interface; approximately pH 7-9; physically associated sulfide phases; rapid aldehyde trapping; free sulfide must not consume all photogenerated holes."}, "mechanism": "Photogenerated holes oxidize the primary alcohol to an aldehyde, while conduction-band electrons support proton reduction and hydrogen evolution. Light supplies the energy for acceptorless dehydrogenation.", "reasoning": "This is the principal speculative redox bottleneck, not an experimentally established prebiotic histidine step. Semiconductor alcohol oxidation coupled to hydrogen evolution motivates the chemistry, but performance of engineered photocatalysts cannot be assumed for natural grains. Mineral rarity, photocorrosion, imidazole oxidation, and poor aldehyde selectivity could invalidate this branch. Molecular oxygen is neither an input nor an implicit oxidant."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Imidazole-4-acetaldehyde imine formation", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia-rich concentrated solution, approximately pH 8-9.5; partial drying and immediate cyanide trapping."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to the aldehyde, and dehydration of the carbinolamine establishes an imine/iminium equilibrium.", "reasoning": "This is standard entry chemistry for a Strecker synthesis (Strecker, 1850). The imine need not accumulate or be isolated because cyanide can trap a small equilibrium population. Its abundance, hydrolysis, and competing reactions with this imidazole-containing aldehyde remain substrate-specific uncertainties."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Cyanide addition to the histidine-precursor imine", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_018"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 8-9.5; retained cyanide inventory; limited irradiation or mineral shading during accumulation."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer, forming the alpha-aminonitrile without stereocontrol.", "reasoning": "The bond-forming pattern is the classical Strecker reaction (Strecker, 1850). Crucially, the aldehyde already has an imidazolylmethyl substituent; using imidazole-4-carbaldehyde instead would give the wrong amino-acid skeleton. Prebiotic availability of the required aldehyde, rather than this generic reaction class, is the major unresolved issue."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Cyanohydrin-first capture of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 7.5-9; cyanide arrives before a large ammonia pulse; cool, partly shaded water limits photodegradation."}, "mechanism": "Reversible cyanide addition to the aldehyde followed by proton transfer forms its cyanohydrin.", "reasoning": "Cyanohydrin formation is established aldehyde chemistry and supplies an operational alternative when cyanide precedes ammonia. Formation or persistence of this specific cyanohydrin in a natural mixture is not established. It is a potential reversible carbonyl reservoir, not evidence for a separate fundamental amino-acid synthesis mechanism."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Ammonia-driven conversion of the cyanohydrin reservoir to aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_018", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "310-350 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia-rich solution, approximately pH 8-9.5; cyanide retained during equilibration."}, "mechanism": "The net conversion proceeds through reversible cyanohydrin dissociation, ammonia capture of the aldehyde, and cyanide trapping of imine/iminium species. Direct substitution of an unactivated hydroxyl group is not assumed.", "reasoning": "This is a chemically defensible cyanohydrin-fed Strecker operation, but substrate-specific prebiotic conversion is unvalidated. It shares transient species with rxn_011 and rxn_012 and therefore represents a different feed-order pathway, not an independent elementary mechanism. Ammonia and cyanide loss can prevent useful conversion."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Histidine aminonitrile hydration to histidinamide", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_019"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions in acidic water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "370-410 K", "pressure": "20-100 bar, maintaining liquid water", "catalyst": "Aqueous hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 2-4 in cooled acidic hydrothermal pore water; transport from surface synthesis is required; avoid the hottest vent fluids."}, "mechanism": "Protonation of the nitrile promotes water addition and tautomerization to the primary amide.", "reasoning": "Nitrile hydration is established chemistry, including the hydrolytic completion of Strecker syntheses. The proposed temperature-acidity window is a candidate geochemical setting, not a measured optimum for this substrate. Aminonitrile decomposition and retro-Strecker chemistry may outcompete hydration, and transport without excessive dilution is unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Histidinamide hydrolysis to histidine", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_022", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions in acidic water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "390-430 K", "pressure": "20-100 bar, maintaining liquid water", "catalyst": "Aqueous hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 2-4; extended but finite residence followed by cooling; ammonia is mainly ammonium in situ."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed addition of water to the amide carbonyl and carbon-nitrogen bond cleavage yield the amino acid and ammonia equivalents.", "reasoning": "Amide hydrolysis completes the conventional Strecker carbon skeleton. Whether hydrolysis is sufficiently rapid under these comparatively dilute natural acidic conditions is an important kinetic gap. Histidine decomposition and racemization constrain residence time; a viable process must cool the product before destructive reactions dominate."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Bucherer-Bergs-type conversion of aminonitrile to histidine hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_020"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ammonium carbonate/bicarbonate buffer"], "conditions": {"temperature": "325-365 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Ammonium carbonate/bicarbonate acid-base medium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 8-9.5; concentrated NH3/CO2-bearing pool; sustained inorganic-carbon supply."}, "mechanism": "Aminonitrile carbamation, cyclization through imino-oxazolidinone-type species, and rearrangement produce a hydantoin. The net atom balance is aminonitrile plus carbon dioxide.", "reasoning": "This invokes the established Bucherer-Bergs reaction family (Bucherer and Steiner, 1934). It offers a chemically distinct downstream reservoir rather than another carbon-skeleton construction. Conversion of this particular imidazole-bearing aminonitrile under natural pool conditions is unvalidated; inorganic-carbon speciation and competing imidazole interactions require testing."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Hydantoin ring opening to N-carbamoylhistidine", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_021"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydroxide in alkaline hydrothermal water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "390-430 K", "pressure": "20-100 bar, maintaining liquid water", "catalyst": "Aqueous hydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 10-11 in a cooled alkaline circulation zone; long residence may be required."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide-assisted attack on the hydantoin carbonyl corresponding to the original nitrile carbon opens the ring to the N-carbamoyl amino acid.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin hydrolysis is established preparative chemistry, but commonly requires more forcing alkalinity than natural alkaline vents provide. Consequently, useful conversion at the stated pH is speculative and is a major bottleneck of these pathways. Alternative ring cleavage, incomplete conversion, and product destruction must be quantified rather than assumed negligible."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "N-Carbamoylhistidine hydrolysis and decarbamoylation", "inputs": ["mol_021", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_022", "mol_004", "mol_006"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions in acidic water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "390-430 K", "pressure": "20-100 bar, maintaining liquid water", "catalyst": "Aqueous hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 2-4 after transport to a separate acidic circulation zone; subsequent cooling is necessary."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic cleavage of the N-carbamoyl group releases histidine; the detached carbamoyl carbon and nitrogen appear ultimately as carbon dioxide and ammonia equivalents.", "reasoning": "Nonenzymatic decarbamoylation is chemically possible, but a productive rate for this substrate under the stated natural conditions is not established. Recyclization to hydantoin and amino-acid degradation are competing processes. The alkaline-to-acidic transfer is required by this proposed hydrolysis sequence and is an explicit geochemical vulnerability, not a decorative environmental connection."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Glycolaldehyde dimer route with imine-first Strecker hydrolysis", "description": "Aldotetrose formation and dehydration supply the dicarbonyl; imidazole construction and hypothetical photodehydrogenation precede imine-first aminonitrile formation and direct hydrolysis. Candidate route, not experimentally demonstrated end to end.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_015", "rxn_016"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_012", "mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_018", "mol_019"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Glycolal