\"InChI=1S/p+1\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe+3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Iron(3+)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ferric iron\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe/q+3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe+2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Iron(2+)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ferrous iron\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe/q+2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolaldehyde self-aldol formation of aldotetroses\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide ion\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-325 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening: pH 9.5-10.5; glycolaldehyde 10-100 mM after evaporation; 0.5-24 h residence; anoxic alkaline pool, with UV shielding by sediment or water depth.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Base-catalyzed enolization followed by aldol addition joins two glycolaldehyde molecules into an aldotetrose skeleton. Erythrose and threose stereoisomers are not separately resolved.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Formose chemistry supports this carbon-carbon bond-forming reaction class (Breslow, 1959). The network does not assume selective erythrose production or removal of other sugars. Continued aldol reactions and rearrangements compete, so useful aldotetrose flux must be measured rather than inferred from atom balance.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolaldehyde-formaldehyde cross-aldol formation of glyceraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide ion\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening: pH 9-10.5; glycolaldehyde 10-100 mM; incremental formaldehyde supply maintaining approximately 1-10 mM; 0.5-12 h residence; anoxic and UV-shielded.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A glycolaldehyde-derived enediolate attacks formaldehyde, extending the chain by one carbon.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a standard elementary transformation within formose networks (Breslow, 1959). Incremental formaldehyde availability is an operational hypothesis for limiting uncontrolled growth, not evidence of selective prebiotic glyceraldehyde accumulation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyceraldehyde-dihydroxyacetone isomerization\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide ion\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-325 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening: pH 8.5-10; total triose concentration 1-100 mM; minutes to hours; reversible reaction represented in the productive direction.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Enediol or enediolate formation interconverts the aldose and ketose.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aldose-ketose interconversion is established chemistry (Lobry de Bruyn and Alberda van Ekenstein, 1895). It produces an equilibrium mixture rather than an isolated dihydroxyacetone stream; competing triose degradation limits residence time.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Dihydroxyacetone hydroxymethylation to erythrulose\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide ion\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-325 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening: pH 9-10.5; dihydroxyacetone 10-100 mM; formaldehyde 1-10 mM supplied incrementally; 0.5-12 h residence.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A dihydroxyacetone enolate adds to formaldehyde to produce a linear ketotetrose.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The connectivity follows established formose aldol chemistry (Breslow, 1959). Other additions, triose isomerization, and degradation compete. No selective mineral templating or quantitative erythrulose production is assumed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrulose-aldotetrose interconversion\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide ion\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-325 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening: pH 8.5-10; tetroses 1-100 mM; minutes to hours; subsequent acidification would stop the alkaline equilibration.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Enediol-mediated carbonyl migration converts the ketotetrose into aldotetrose stereoisomers.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The reaction class is established (Lobry de Bruyn and Alberda van Ekenstein, 1895), but the equilibrium does not guarantee aldotetrose enrichment. This route is retained as a chemically distinct chain-extension sequence, not as a claim of superior yield.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyceraldehyde dehydration to methylglyoxal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide ion\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening: pH 8-9.5; glyceraldehyde 1-100 mM; 0.5-24 h residence; anoxic, protected from direct UV.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Triose enolization and dehydration generate the alpha-dicarbonyl methylglyoxal.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Methylglyoxal formation belongs to established triose degradation chemistry. Formation is not equivalent to accumulation: hydration, oligomerization, and rearrangement to lactate can compete. The proposed window is not supported here by substrate-specific yield or rate measurements.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Methylglyoxal hydroxymethylation to 3-deoxytetrosone\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide ion\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening: pH 8.5-9.5; methylglyoxal 1-20 mM; formaldehyde 1-10 mM; 0.1-6 h residence, followed by rapid reduction of alkalinity.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Enolization at the methyl group followed by addition to formaldehyde gives OHC-CO-CH2-CH2OH.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a chemically coherent but substrate-selectivity-unverified aldol proposal. It provides an alternative to aldotetrose dehydration without changing the required carbon skeleton. Hydration, oligomerization, and intramolecular rearrangement may dominate; no supplied research establishes productive flux through this step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldotetrose dehydration to 3-deoxytetrosone\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ion\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"345-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening: pH 3-5; total aldotetrose 10-100 mM; 0.5-24 h residence in an acidified geothermal pool; exclude cyanide during this stage.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Enolization, dehydration, and carbonyl rearrangement formally convert the aldotetrose into its 3-deoxyosone.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Deoxyosones are established products of sugar-degradation and Maillard-related chemistry, reviewed by Ledl and Schleicher (1990). That general literature does not establish selective, amine-free production of this particular tetrose-derived intermediate under these conditions. This remains an upstream bottleneck rather than a validated preparation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Debus-type construction of the hydroxyethyl-substituted imidazole\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening: pH 8-9; dicarbonyl 1-10 mM; ammonia/ammonium inventory 0.05-0.5 M; formaldehyde 1-10 mM supplied incrementally; 1-48 h residence. Cyanide and dissolved transition metals are withheld.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The alpha-dicarbonyl, formaldehyde, and two ammonia equivalents undergo imine formation, ring closure, and dehydration to an imidazole. Three water equivalents are released.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Debus-type imidazole formation is experimentally established as a reaction family (Debus, 1858). It does not demonstrate selective production from this dicarbonyl in a prebiotic sugar mixture. Formaldehyde-ammonia condensation, alternative heterocycles, and polymer formation are substantial competitors. Target-specific identification and yield measurements are essential.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid mobilization of ferric iron from goethite\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening: acidic volcanic runoff, pH 1-2.5; finely divided goethite; hours to days; seek 0.5-10 mM dissolved ferric iron. Ferric-mineral availability is an explicit feedstock assumption.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proton-promoted dissolution consumes three proton equivalents per FeOOH and releases ferric iron and two water equivalents.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Acid dissolution is ordinary mineral chemistry, although crystalline goethite may dissolve slowly. A ferric mineral is not evidence for atmospheric oxygen: anoxic Fe(II) photooxidation is a possible source of limited ferric inventories (Braterman et al., 1983). Formation and abundance of the required deposit are not demonstrated here; the surrounding system can remain predominantly ferrous.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional ferric-acceptor photooxidation to imidazoleacetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_021\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Anatase, TiO2\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Anatase semiconductor surface\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening: pH 2-3; alcohol 0.1-1 mM; ferric iron 0.5-10 mM; anatase suspension 0.1-1 g/L; sunlight with an approximately 335-385 nm component of 1-5 mW/cm2; sample over 5-120 min. Exclude sulfide and cyanide. After irradiation, raise pH to precipitate iron and assess whether settling and overflow can recover the aldehyde without excessive dilution or adsorption loss.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Anatase photoexcitation generates electron-hole pairs. Surface holes can oxidize the alcohol to an aldehyde, while two ferric-ion equivalents accept the two electrons and become ferrous iron. Two proton equivalents are released; molecular oxygen and hydrogen evolution are unnecessary.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Semiconductor photoredox chemistry supports the general charge-transfer architecture, not this selective synthesis (Hoffmann et al., 1995). This replacement makes the oxidant, catalyst, light exposure, and electron balance explicit. Nevertheless, imidazole oxidation, aldehyde overoxidation, radical damage, and recombination may prevent useful conversion. No substrate-specific rate, selectivity, or quantum yield is available here. This remains a route-blocking hypothesis until aldehyde formation and survival are measured against those losses.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Imidazoleacetaldehyde-ammonia imine equilibrium\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening: pH 8-9; aldehyde 0.1-10 mM; ammonia/ammonium 0.05-0.5 M; minutes to hours; use a shaded receiving pool only after iron removal. No isolated imine is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible carbinolamine formation and dehydration establish an aldehyde-imine equilibrium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the conventional imine channel of Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850). In water, the imine may remain a minor species; subsequent cyanide trapping, rather than quantitative dehydration, must pull the equilibrium. Residual reactive carbonyls and iron could divert reagents, so operational staging is necessary but not yet validated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide capture of the aldimine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening: pH 8-9; total HCN/cyanide 1-50 mM; ammonia-rich solution from rxn_012; 0.5-24 h residence; shallow but UV-shielded, with limited gas exchange to reduce HCN loss.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide addition to the imine or iminium carbon forms the alpha-aminonitrile without stereocontrol.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The bond construction is established Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850), and its connectivity gives the required histidine skeleton. Actual conversion of this aldehyde-derived imine under the stated environmental conditions is not established here. Cyanohydrin formation, cyanide volatilization, and metal-cyanide complexation compete.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible cyanohydrin capture of imidazoleacetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cyanide ion acting as the basic component of the HCN/cyanide pair\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"HCN/cyanide acid-base pair\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening: pH 7.5-8.5; aldehyde 0.1-10 mM; total HCN/cyanide 1-50 mM; minutes to hours; low dissolved iron; UV-shielded pool.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl and proton transfer produces the cyanohydrin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanohydrin formation is a well-established aldehyde reaction. Whether this particular cyanohydrin provides storage rather than irreversible loss is unmeasured. It is represented as an alternative capture channel within coupled Strecker equilibria, not as an independently validated histidine synthesis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Equilibrium-mediated conversion of cyanohydrin to aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening: pH 8-9; ammonia/ammonium 0.05-0.5 M; cyanohydrin 0.1-10 mM; retain the equilibrating HCN/cyanide pool; 1-48 h residence; shield from UV.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanohydrin dissociation replenishes aldehyde and cyanide; aldehyde reacts with ammonia, and cyanide traps the resulting imine. The displayed equation is the net transformation, not direct displacement of hydroxide by ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is consistent with coupled cyanohydrin and Strecker equilibria (Strecker, 1850). It does not establish a separate elementary substitution or improved yield. Productive release and trapping must outpace cyanohydrin degradation and HCN loss.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-catalyzed histidinenitrile hydration\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ion\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"345-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening: approximately 0.3-3 M acid equivalents in concentrated volcanic chloride-rich water; aminonitrile 0.1-10 mM; sample over 6 h to 7 days. Acidify only after the cyanide-capture episode; quantify HCN release and aminonitrile decomposition.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile protonation, water addition, and tautomerization produce the primary amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Acid-catalyzed nitrile hydration is established chemistry, but a useful operating window for histidinenitrile is not supplied. Concentrated acidity is a restrictive environmental requirement, and alpha-aminonitrile decomposition may compete. The sampling interval is an experimental search range, not a prediction of completion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-catalyzed histidinamide hydrolysis to histidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ion\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"355-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening: approximately 1-6 M acid equivalents in a highly concentrated volcanic pool; amide 0.1-10 mM; 1-14 days with water replenishment as needed; UV-shielded. Ammonia is present mainly as ammonium under these conditions.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water attacks the protonated amide carbonyl; tetrahedral-intermediate collapse releases ammonia/ammonium and gives histidine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amide hydrolysis is chemically standard, and the imidazole ring is generally compatible with acidic amino-acid hydrolysis. That does not establish histidinamide conversion and histidine survival at these exact conditions. The route is acceptable only if measured hydrolysis outpaces deamination, decomposition, adsorption, and evaporation losses. No such kinetic comparison is claimed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline histidinenitrile hydration\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide ion\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"345-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening: pH 11.5-12.2 in a carbonate-poor alkaline spring pool; aminonitrile 0.1-10 mM; 6 h to 14 days; low dissolved transition metals and UV shielding. This pH requires a locally maintained alkalinity source.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide-mediated attack on the nitrile followed by proton transfers yields the amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Alkaline hydration is a genuine alternative hydrolysis chemistry, included because it avoids concentrated volcanic acid. It is not a distinct carbon-skeleton synthesis. Rates for this aminonitrile and competition with decomposition are unknown; the required sustained alkalinity is geographically restrictive.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline histidinamide hydrolysis to histidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide ion\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"355-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide-mediated hydrolysis; alkaline buffering is required\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening: pH 11.5-12.2; amide 0.1-10 mM; 1-14 days; carbonate-poor alkaline water, protected from UV. Histidine is predominantly anionic; the node records its canonical neutral composition. Maintaining pH requires base inventory as carboxylate accumulates.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide addition and tetrahedral-intermediate collapse cleave the amide, producing histidinate and ammonia in situ; neutral histidine is the network bookkeeping form.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Base-mediated amide cleavage is established chemistry, but productive histidinamide processing under this proposed surface window is unverified. Ammonia escape can favor conversion, while prolonged heating can increase organic losses. Racemization would not invalidate a racemic target, but degradation would. This branch requires its own yield and survival measurements and is not assumed equivalent to acid hydrolysis.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct aldotetrose assembly, imine capture, acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Glycolaldehyde self-aldol chemistry supplies the tetrose, followed by conditional deoxyosone and imidazole formation, ferric-acceptor photooxidation, imine-channel Strecker capture, and acidic nitrile/amide hydrolysis. This is an unvalidated candidate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct aldotetrose assembly, cyanohydrin capture, acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Uses the direct tetrose entry route but initially captures the aldehyde as its cyanohydrin. Ammonia-mediated equilibration supplies aminonitrile for acidic hydrolysis. Cyanohydrin storage is not assumed to improve yield.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct aldotetrose assembly, imine capture, alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Uses the direct tetrose and imine-capture sequence, followed by alkaline rather than acidic processing. It is conditionally distinct from P1, not an independent carbon-skeleton synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct aldotetrose assembly, cyanohydrin capture, alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Combines direct tetrose assembly, reversible cyanohydrin capture, and alkaline hydrolysis. Both cyanohydrin release and the final alkaline survival window require validation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Ketotetrose chain extension, imine capture, acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"A glycolaldehyde-formaldehyde cross-aldol produces glyceraldehyde; ketose isomerization and a second hydroxymethylation produce erythrulose, which accesses the aldotetrose pool. The downstream imine/acid sequence is shared with P1.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Ketotetrose chain extension, cyanohydrin capture, acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Uses the ketotetrose chain-extension entry route and cyanohydrin-mediated Strecker equilibration before acidic hydrolysis. Sugar-equilibrium mixtures are carried forward rather than assumed to be purified.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Ketotetrose chain extension, imine capture, alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Combines the ketotetrose entry route with imine-channel capture and alkaline hydrolysis. No advantage over direct tetrose assembly or acidic processing is asserted.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Ketotetrose chain extension, cyanohydrin capture, alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Uses ketotetrose-derived aldotetroses, the shared conditional imidazole/aldehyde module, cyanohydrin capture, and alkaline hydrolysis. It retains all common selectivity and survival bottlenecks.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Methylglyoxal chain extension, imine capture, acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyceraldehyde degradation supplies methylglyoxal, whose proposed hydroxymethylation bypasses aldotetrose dehydration. The imine-channel Strecker sequence and acidic hydrolysis then lead formally to histidine. Selective methylglyoxal hydroxymethylation is an additional unresolved bottleneck.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Methylglyoxal chain extension, cyanohydrin capture, acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Combines the methylglyoxal entry route with cyanohydrin-mediated Strecker equilibration and acidic hydrolysis. This is the tenth enumerated candidate combination, not evidence for ten independently demonstrated syntheses.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_016\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_013\",\n    \"mol_016\",\n    \"mol_017\",\n    \"mol_018\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_017\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_011\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_012\", \"rxn_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_016\", \"rxn_018\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_016\", \"rxn_018\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_017\", \"rxn_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_017\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Histidine, racemic", "overall_strategy": "This is a connected, explicitly conditional research network, not a demonstrated complete prebiotic synthesis. Ten distinct, condition-resolved pathways are enumerated by combining three carbon-skeleton assembly routes, two Strecker capture channels, and acid/base hydrolysis alternatives. These are not ten independently established prebiotic syntheses. The network replaces unsupported acceptorless alcohol dehydrogenation with an explicitly electron-balanced, anatase-assisted photooxidation consuming ferric iron; neither molecular oxygen nor unexplained hydrogen evolution is required. However, selective oxidation of the actual imidazole-containing substrate remains unverified. Selective production of the substituted imidazole and hydrolysis faster than substrate degradation are additional unresolved bottlenecks. All numerical operating windows are proposed screening conditions, not reported substrate-specific performance. Processing occurs in a surface-pool system through successive concentration, irradiation, precipitation, overflow, and pH-change episodes; no hydrothermal transport is assumed. Organic intermediates are not assumed to be purified automatically between episodes. Mineral adsorption losses, dilution, competing reactions, and reagent availability must be measured. Molecule nodes use canonical neutral organic structures; protonation, hydration, and imidazole tautomerism are not separately expanded. No enantioenrichment is assumed. The network therefore does not establish high-confidence histidine production or resolve the requested chemistry merely by providing ten paths.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "Methanal", "common_name": "Formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "2-Hydroxyethanal", "common_name": "Glycolaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,4H,2H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "Azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Oxidane", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C4H8O4", "iupac_name": "2,3,4-Trihydroxybutanal", "common_name": "Aldotetrose, stereochemistry unspecified", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C3H6O3", "iupac_name": "2,3-Dihydroxypropanal", "common_name": "Glyceraldehyde, racemic", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c4-1-3(6)2-5/h1,3,5-6H,2H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C3H6O3", "iupac_name": "1,3-Dihydroxypropan-2-one", "common_name": "Dihydroxyacetone", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c4-1-3(6)2-5/h4-5H,1-2H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C4H8O4", "iupac_name": "1,3,4-Trihydroxybutan-2-one", "common_name": "Erythrulose, racemic", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C3H4O2", "iupac_name": "2-Oxopropanal", "common_name": "Methylglyoxal", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O2/c1-3(5)2-4/h2H,1H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C4H6O3", "iupac_name": "4-Hydroxy-2-oxobutanal", "common_name": "3-Deoxytetrosone", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C5H8N2O", "iupac_name": "2-(1H-Imidazol-4-yl)ethan-1-ol", "common_name": "4-(2-Hydroxyethyl)imidazole", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C5H6N2O", "iupac_name": "2-(1H-Imidazol-4-yl)ethanal", "common_name": "Imidazole-4-acetaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C5H7N3", "iupac_name": "2-(1H-Imidazol-4-yl)ethanimine", "common_name": "Imidazoleacetaldehyde-derived aldimine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C6H7N3O", "iupac_name": "2-Hydroxy-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanenitrile", "common_name": "Imidazoleacetaldehyde cyanohydrin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C6H8N4", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanenitrile", "common_name": "Histidinenitrile, racemic", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C6H10N4O", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanamide", "common_name": "Histidinamide, racemic", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C6H9N3O2", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanoic acid", "common_name": "Histidine, racemic", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C6H9N3O2/c7-5(6(10)11)1-4-2-8-3-9-4/h2-3,5H,1,7H2,(H,8,9)(H,10,11)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "FeHO2", "iupac_name": "Iron(III) oxide hydroxide", "common_name": "Goethite", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "Hydron", "common_name": "Proton", "inchi": "InChI=1S/p+1", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "Fe+3", "iupac_name": "Iron(3+)", "common_name": "Ferric iron", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "Fe+2", "iupac_name": "Iron(2+)", "common_name": "Ferrous iron", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Glycolaldehyde self-aldol formation of aldotetroses", "inputs": ["mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide ion"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydroxide ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening: pH 9.5-10.5; glycolaldehyde 10-100 mM after evaporation; 0.5-24 h residence; anoxic alkaline pool, with UV shielding by sediment or water depth."}, "mechanism": "Base-catalyzed enolization followed by aldol addition joins two glycolaldehyde molecules into an aldotetrose skeleton. Erythrose and threose stereoisomers are not separately resolved.", "reasoning": "Formose chemistry supports this carbon-carbon bond-forming reaction class (Breslow, 1959). The network does not assume selective erythrose production or removal of other sugars. Continued aldol reactions and rearrangements compete, so useful aldotetrose flux must be measured rather than inferred from atom balance."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Glycolaldehyde-formaldehyde cross-aldol formation of glyceraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide ion"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydroxide ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening: pH 9-10.5; glycolaldehyde 10-100 mM; incremental formaldehyde supply maintaining approximately 1-10 mM; 0.5-12 h residence; anoxic and UV-shielded."}, "mechanism": "A glycolaldehyde-derived enediolate attacks formaldehyde, extending the chain by one carbon.", "reasoning": "This is a standard elementary transformation within formose networks (Breslow, 1959). Incremental formaldehyde availability is an operational hypothesis for limiting uncontrolled growth, not evidence of selective prebiotic glyceraldehyde accumulation."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Glyceraldehyde-dihydroxyacetone isomerization", "inputs": ["mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide ion"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydroxide ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening: pH 8.5-10; total triose concentration 1-100 mM; minutes to hours; reversible reaction represented in the productive direction."}, "mechanism": "Enediol or enediolate formation interconverts the aldose and ketose.", "reasoning": "Aldose-ketose interconversion is established chemistry (Lobry de Bruyn and Alberda van Ekenstein, 1895). It produces an equilibrium mixture rather than an isolated dihydroxyacetone stream; competing triose degradation limits residence time."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Dihydroxyacetone hydroxymethylation to erythrulose", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide ion"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydroxide ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening: pH 9-10.5; dihydroxyacetone 10-100 mM; formaldehyde 1-10 mM supplied incrementally; 0.5-12 h residence."}, "mechanism": "A dihydroxyacetone enolate adds to formaldehyde to produce a linear ketotetrose.", "reasoning": "The connectivity follows established formose aldol chemistry (Breslow, 1959). Other additions, triose isomerization, and degradation compete. No selective mineral templating or quantitative erythrulose production is assumed."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Erythrulose-aldotetrose interconversion", "inputs": ["mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide ion"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydroxide ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening: pH 8.5-10; tetroses 1-100 mM; minutes to hours; subsequent acidification would stop the alkaline equilibration."}, "mechanism": "Enediol-mediated carbonyl migration converts the ketotetrose into aldotetrose stereoisomers.", "reasoning": "The reaction class is established (Lobry de Bruyn and Alberda van Ekenstein, 1895), but the equilibrium does not guarantee aldotetrose enrichment. This route is retained as a chemically distinct chain-extension sequence, not as a claim of superior yield."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Glyceraldehyde dehydration to methylglyoxal", "inputs": ["mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide ion"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydroxide ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening: pH 8-9.5; glyceraldehyde 1-100 mM; 0.5-24 h residence; anoxic, protected from direct UV."}, "mechanism": "Triose enolization and dehydration generate the alpha-dicarbonyl methylglyoxal.", "reasoning": "Methylglyoxal formation belongs to established triose degradation chemistry. Formation is not equivalent to accumulation: hydration, oligomerization, and rearrangement to lactate can compete. The proposed window is not supported here by substrate-specific yield or rate measurements."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Methylglyoxal hydroxymethylation to 3-deoxytetrosone", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide ion"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydroxide ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening: pH 8.5-9.5; methylglyoxal 1-20 mM; formaldehyde 1-10 mM; 0.1-6 h residence, followed by rapid reduction of alkalinity."}, "mechanism": "Enolization at the methyl group followed by addition to formaldehyde gives OHC-CO-CH2-CH2OH.", "reasoning": "This is a chemically coherent but substrate-selectivity-unverified aldol proposal. It provides an alternative to aldotetrose dehydration without changing the required carbon skeleton. Hydration, oligomerization, and intramolecular rearrangement may dominate; no supplied research establishes productive flux through this step."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Aldotetrose dehydration to 3-deoxytetrosone", "inputs": ["mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ion"], "conditions": {"temperature": "345-365 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening: pH 3-5; total aldotetrose 10-100 mM; 0.5-24 h residence in an acidified geothermal pool; exclude cyanide during this stage."}, "mechanism": "Enolization, dehydration, and carbonyl rearrangement formally convert the aldotetrose into its 3-deoxyosone.", "reasoning": "Deoxyosones are established products of sugar-degradation and Maillard-related chemistry, reviewed by Ledl and Schleicher (1990). That general literature does not establish selective, amine-free production of this particular tetrose-derived intermediate under these conditions. This remains an upstream bottleneck rather than a validated preparation."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Debus-type construction of the hydroxyethyl-substituted imidazole", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening: pH 8-9; dicarbonyl 1-10 mM; ammonia/ammonium inventory 0.05-0.5 M; formaldehyde 1-10 mM supplied incrementally; 1-48 h residence. Cyanide and dissolved transition metals are withheld."}, "mechanism": "The alpha-dicarbonyl, formaldehyde, and two ammonia equivalents undergo imine formation, ring closure, and dehydration to an imidazole. Three water equivalents are released.", "reasoning": "Debus-type imidazole formation is experimentally established as a reaction family (Debus, 1858). It does not demonstrate selective production from this dicarbonyl in a prebiotic sugar mixture. Formaldehyde-ammonia condensation, alternative heterocycles, and polymer formation are substantial competitors. Target-specific identification and yield measurements are essential."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Acid mobilization of ferric iron from goethite", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_020"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening: acidic volcanic runoff, pH 1-2.5; finely divided goethite; hours to days; seek 0.5-10 mM dissolved ferric iron. Ferric-mineral availability is an explicit feedstock assumption."}, "mechanism": "Proton-promoted dissolution consumes three proton equivalents per FeOOH and releases ferric iron and two water equivalents.", "reasoning": "Acid dissolution is ordinary mineral chemistry, although crystalline goethite may dissolve slowly. A ferric mineral is not evidence for atmospheric oxygen: anoxic Fe(II) photooxidation is a possible source of limited ferric inventories (Braterman et al., 1983). Formation and abundance of the required deposit are not demonstrated here; the surrounding system can remain predominantly ferrous."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Conditional ferric-acceptor photooxidation to imidazoleacetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_021"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_022", "mol_020"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Anatase, TiO2"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Anatase semiconductor surface", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening: pH 2-3; alcohol 0.1-1 mM; ferric iron 0.5-10 mM; anatase suspension 0.1-1 g/L; sunlight with an approximately 335-385 nm component of 1-5 mW/cm2; sample over 5-120 min. Exclude sulfide and cyanide. After irradiation, raise pH to precipitate iron and assess whether settling and overflow can recover the aldehyde without excessive dilution or adsorption loss."}, "mechanism": "Anatase photoexcitation generates electron-hole pairs. Surface holes can oxidize the alcohol to an aldehyde, while two ferric-ion equivalents accept the two electrons and become ferrous iron. Two proton equivalents are released; molecular oxygen and hydrogen evolution are unnecessary.", "reasoning": "Semiconductor photoredox chemistry supports the general charge-transfer architecture, not this selective synthesis (Hoffmann et al., 1995). This replacement makes the oxidant, catalyst, light exposure, and electron balance explicit. Nevertheless, imidazole oxidation, aldehyde overoxidation, radical damage, and recombination may prevent useful conversion. No substrate-specific rate, selectivity, or quantum yield is available here. This remains a route-blocking hypothesis until aldehyde formation and survival are measured against those losses."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Imidazoleacetaldehyde-ammonia imine equilibrium", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening: pH 8-9; aldehyde 0.1-10 mM; ammonia/ammonium 0.05-0.5 M; minutes to hours; use a shaded receiving pool only after iron removal. No isolated imine is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Reversible carbinolamine formation and dehydration establish an aldehyde-imine equilibrium.", "reasoning": "This is the conventional imine channel of Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850). In water, the imine may remain a minor species; subsequent cyanide trapping, rather than quantitative dehydration, must pull the equilibrium. Residual reactive carbonyls and iron could divert reagents, so operational staging is necessary but not yet validated."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Cyanide capture of the aldimine", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_016"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening: pH 8-9; total HCN/cyanide 1-50 mM; ammonia-rich solution from rxn_012; 0.5-24 h residence; shallow but UV-shielded, with limited gas exchange to reduce HCN loss."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide addition to the imine or iminium carbon forms the alpha-aminonitrile without stereocontrol.", "reasoning": "The bond construction is established Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850), and its connectivity gives the required histidine skeleton. Actual conversion of this aldehyde-derived imine under the stated environmental conditions is not established here. Cyanohydrin formation, cyanide volatilization, and metal-cyanide complexation compete."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Reversible cyanohydrin capture of imidazoleacetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cyanide ion acting as the basic component of the HCN/cyanide pair"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "HCN/cyanide acid-base pair", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening: pH 7.5-8.5; aldehyde 0.1-10 mM; total HCN/cyanide 1-50 mM; minutes to hours; low dissolved iron; UV-shielded pool."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl and proton transfer produces the cyanohydrin.", "reasoning": "Cyanohydrin formation is a well-established aldehyde reaction. Whether this particular cyanohydrin provides storage rather than irreversible loss is unmeasured. It is represented as an alternative capture channel within coupled Strecker equilibria, not as an independently validated histidine synthesis."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Equilibrium-mediated conversion of cyanohydrin to aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening: pH 8-9; ammonia/ammonium 0.05-0.5 M; cyanohydrin 0.1-10 mM; retain the equilibrating HCN/cyanide pool; 1-48 h residence; shield from UV."}, "mechanism": "Cyanohydrin dissociation replenishes aldehyde and cyanide; aldehyde reacts with ammonia, and cyanide traps the resulting imine. The displayed equation is the net transformation, not direct displacement of hydroxide by ammonia.", "reasoning": "This is consistent with coupled cyanohydrin and Strecker equilibria (Strecker, 1850). It does not establish a separate elementary substitution or improved yield. Productive release and trapping must outpace cyanohydrin degradation and HCN loss."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Acid-catalyzed histidinenitrile hydration", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ion"], "conditions": {"temperature": "345-365 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening: approximately 0.3-3 M acid equivalents in concentrated volcanic chloride-rich water; aminonitrile 0.1-10 mM; sample over 6 h to 7 days. Acidify only after the cyanide-capture episode; quantify HCN release and aminonitrile decomposition."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile protonation, water addition, and tautomerization produce the primary amide.", "reasoning": "Acid-catalyzed nitrile hydration is established chemistry, but a useful operating window for histidinenitrile is not supplied. Concentrated acidity is a restrictive environmental requirement, and alpha-aminonitrile decomposition may compete. The sampling interval is an experimental search range, not a prediction of completion."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Acid-catalyzed histidinamide hydrolysis to histidine", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_018", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ion"], "conditions": {"temperature": "355-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening: approximately 1-6 M acid equivalents in a highly concentrated volcanic pool; amide 0.1-10 mM; 1-14 days with water replenishment as needed; UV-shielded. Ammonia is present mainly as ammonium under these conditions."}, "mechanism": "Water attacks the protonated amide carbonyl; tetrahedral-intermediate collapse releases ammonia/ammonium and gives histidine.", "reasoning": "Amide hydrolysis is chemically standard, and the imidazole ring is generally compatible with acidic amino-acid hydrolysis. That does not establish histidinamide conversion and histidine survival at these exact conditions. The route is acceptable only if measured hydrolysis outpaces deamination, decomposition, adsorption, and evaporation losses. No such kinetic comparison is claimed."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Alkaline histidinenitrile hydration", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide ion"], "conditions": {"temperature": "345-365 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydroxide ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening: pH 11.5-12.2 in a carbonate-poor alkaline spring pool; aminonitrile 0.1-10 mM; 6 h to 14 days; low dissolved transition metals and UV shielding. This pH requires a locally maintained alkalinity source."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide-mediated attack on the nitrile followed by proton transfers yields the amide.", "reasoning": "Alkaline hydration is a genuine alternative hydrolysis chemistry, included because it avoids concentrated volcanic acid. It is not a distinct carbon-skeleton synthesis. Rates for this aminonitrile and competition with decomposition are unknown; the required sustained alkalinity is geographically restrictive."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "Alkaline histidinamide hydrolysis to histidine", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_018", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide ion"], "conditions": {"temperature": "355-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydroxide-mediated hydrolysis; alkaline buffering is required", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening: pH 11.5-12.2; amide 0.1-10 mM; 1-14 days; carbonate-poor alkaline water, protected from UV. Histidine is predominantly anionic; the node records its canonical neutral composition. Maintaining pH requires base inventory as carboxylate accumulates."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide addition and tetrahedral-intermediate collapse cleave the amide, producing histidinate and ammonia in situ; neutral histidine is the network bookkeeping form.", "reasoning": "Base-mediated amide cleavage is established chemistry, but productive histidinamide processing under this proposed surface window is unverified. Ammonia escape can favor conversion, while prolonged heating can increase organic losses. Racemization would not invalidate a racemic target, but degradation would. This branch requires its own yield and survival measurements and is not assumed equivalent to acid hydrolysis."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Direct aldotetrose assembly, imine capture, acidic hydrolysis", "description": "Glycolaldehyde self-aldol chemistry supplies the tetrose, followed by conditional deoxyosone and imidazole formation, ferric-acceptor photooxidation, imine-channel Strecker capture, and acidic nitrile/amide hydrolysis. This is an unvalidated candidate.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_013", "rxn_016", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_014", "mol_016", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Direct aldotetrose assembly, cyanohydrin capture, acidic hydrolysis", "description": "Uses the direct tetrose entry route but initially captures the aldehyde as its cyanohydrin. Ammonia-mediated equilibration supplies aminonitrile for acidic hydrolysis. Cyanohydrin storage is not assumed to improve yield.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_014", "rxn_015", "rxn_016", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Direct aldotetrose assembly, imine capture, alkaline hydrolysis", "description": "Uses the direct tetrose and imine-capture sequence, followed by alkaline rather than acidic processing. It is conditionally distinct from P1, not an independent carbon-skeleton synthesis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_013", "rxn_018", "rxn_019"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_011", "mol_013", "mol_014", "mol_016", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Direct aldotetrose assembly, cyanohydrin capture, alkaline hydrolysis", "description": "Combines direct tetrose assembly, reversible cyanohydrin capture, and alkaline hydrolysis. Both cyanohydrin release and the final alkaline survival window require validation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_014", "rxn_015", "rxn_018", "rxn_019"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_011", "mol_013", "mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Ketotetrose chain extension, imine capture, acidic hydrolysis", "description": "A glycolaldehyde-formaldehyde cross-aldol produces glyceraldehyde; ketose isomerization and a second hydroxymethylation produce erythrulose, which accesses the aldotetrose pool. The downstream imine/acid sequence is shared with P1.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_013", "rxn_016", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_009