tlenecks are selective substituted-imidazole production in rxn_009 and selective photooxidation in rxn_011, with route-specific uncertainty in rxn_007 or rxn_008. Final nitrile/amide hydrolysis is chemically credible, but substrate-specific kinetics and integrated histidine recovery are absent. Qualitative surface-pool staging is physically conceivable; reaction-resolved pH, temperature, duration, irradiation, mineral loading and acid/base inventories are not supplied. Ferric mobilization, residual iron binding cyanide, organic adsorption or coprecipitation, dilution and volatile reagent losses require measured budgets. Neutral organic representations are acceptable for the stated balance convention, but operational speciation still matters. No citations or substrate-specific results are supplied to verify literature alignment. Problematic reaction lists identify validation needs, not established conservation failures. P1 is a provisional best representative, tied with P3 because the evidence does not distinguish acid from base hydrolysis. Confidence reflects unresolved chemistry and integration, not pathway count, convergence, feedstock preference or environment order. This remains a plausible research proposal rather than an approved high-confidence complete synthesis."}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 255547.86920547485, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T02:54:00.678307", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Histidine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.4\n- Feedback: Structural validity is high: the 22-molecule, 19-reaction network is connected, molecule IDs appear consistent, and the named intermediates provide the correct connectivity for DL-histidine. The supplied deterministic check establishes conservation for all reactions. rxn_001 requires two glycolaldehydes, and rxn_009 requires two ammonias and produces three waters; these are coefficient omissions, not conservation violations. The central limitation is operational chemistry. Debus-type imidazole formation is plausible in general, but selective formation of mol_013 from the proposed mixture is not established. rxn_010 is a balanced, formally photon-driven acceptorless dehydrogenation, but no photocatalyst, irradiation conditions, hydrogen-evolution performance, or substrate-specific selectivity is supplied. It is therefore an unresolved route-blocking conversion, not a mass-balance error. Both final-processing branches need demonstrated hydrolysis conditions that outpace intermediate and histidine degradation; the hydantoin branch has additional demanding cleavage chemistry. Surface and Hydrothermal labels do not specify temperature, pressure, pH, concentrations, residence times, catalyst availability, or illumination sufficiently to establish environmental consistency. Transfer is physically conceivable but requires a defined transport and dilution scenario. Coexistence of reactive carbonyls, ammonia, and cyanide also requires evidence for productive selectivity or operational staging. No research findings or citations were supplied, so substrate-specific experimental support cannot be verified; the explicit acknowledgment of hypothetical status is appropriate. Overall confidence reflects the unresolved common conversions, not an arithmetic average of individually plausible steps or a reward for pathway count. P1 is a representative of the tied highest-scoring P1/P2 alternatives, not a demonstrated optimum. For P9/P10, substituting cyanohydrin-first capture for the assumed imine-first branch would not materially change the evaluation. The network is not approved at the required 0.9 confidence threshold.\n- Issues: ['The carbon skeleton and functional-group sequence correctly connect glycolaldehyde to racemic histidine. Glycolaldehyde self-aldol chemistry and Strecker chemistry are plausible reaction classes, but selective production of the required deoxytetrosone and substituted imidazole is unestablished here. The principal route-blocking uncertainty is rxn_010: balanced alcohol dehydrogenation does not establish selective aldehyde formation and hydrogen evolution under unspecified surface photochemistry. Hydrothermal nitrile and amide hydrolysis also require a demonstrated operating window that preserves the substrate and histidine.', 'Cyanohydrin formation is a plausible reservoir for the aldehyde, and rxn_014 is reasonable as an equilibrium-mediated net Strecker transformation rather than direct displacement of hydroxide by ammonia. No evidence supplied establishes better or worse overall performance than the imine-first representation. This route retains the same substituted-imidazole, photodehydrogenation, and final-hydrolysis uncertainties as P1.', 'The hydantoin branch is structurally coherent, and aminonitrile plus carbon dioxide is a balanced net representation of hydantoin formation. However, a Bucherer-Bergs-type reaction requires suitable carbonate/ammonia speciation and demonstrated conversion for this substrate. Hydantoin opening and subsequent decarbamoylation can require demanding conditions; neither their rates nor compatibility with histidine survival is established by the Hydrothermal label. The common rxn_010 bottleneck remains unresolved.', 'The cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile sequence can feed a hydantoin pathway through the acknowledged Strecker equilibria. This is a coherent operational alternative, not an independently validated mechanism. Productive flux through cyanohydrin release, aminonitrile formation, and carbon dioxide incorporation requires compatible solution speciation. The unvalidated aldehyde-forming step and demanding hydantoin processing prevent confidence in complete target production.', 'Methylglyoxal hydroxymethylation has the correct formal connectivity to produce the required four-carbon dicarbonyl. Its productive selectivity is nevertheless uncertain because methylglyoxal hydration, oligomerization, and base-promoted rearrangement can compete with the proposed aldol chemistry. The network provides no operating conditions or yields resolving those competitions. Subsequent imidazole formation, anoxic oxidation, and hydrolysis remain the principal shared limitations.', 'The cyanohydrin-first representation is chemically reasonable at the level of reversible Strecker chemistry, but does not resolve selective methylglyoxal chain extension or aldehyde production. Its usefulness depends on whether cyanohydrin storage remains reversible and supplies aminonitrile at useful concentrations under the proposed conditions. The final product identity is correct, but continuous production through the entire sequence is unsupported.', 'This route combines uncertain methylglyoxal hydroxymethylation with a plausible but unvalidated histidine-hydantoin sequence. The concern is not the number of steps: it is unresolved dicarbonyl selectivity, lack of an established anoxic oxidation system, and potentially incompatible conditions for hydantoin opening and decarbamoylation. Atom conservation and correct target connectivity do not establish these conversions kinetically.', 'The sequence is formally connected and balanced. Cyanohydrin storage and hydantoin formation could provide operational capture stages, but neither enrichment nor improved survival is demonstrated for these intermediates. Productive passage through their coupled equilibria remains conditional on pH and reagent concentrations. Methylglyoxal selectivity, rxn_010, and final hydantoin processing remain unresolved.', 'Triose isomerization, dihydroxyacetone hydroxymethylation, and ketose-aldose interconversion are plausible reaction classes. They generate equilibrium mixtures rather than necessarily delivering an enriched aldotetrose stream, so productive capture into rxn_008 needs evidence. Racemic and stereochemically mixed sugar precursors are not intrinsically incompatible with the stated racemic target. The unspecified Strecker capture branch is scored here as rxn_011/rxn_012. The principal oxidation and hydrolysis limitations remain unchanged.', 'The ketotetrose route has plausible upstream chemistry, but selective passage from equilibrating s