ollows conventional Strecker synthesis (Strecker, 1850). This does not establish histidinenitrile production in the proposed mixed feed. Cyanohydrin formation is an accompanying equilibrium, not counted here as an independently validated pathway. Useful flux requires cyanide capture to compete successfully with aldehyde loss, iron complexation, and aminonitrile degradation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate selective imination before imidazole assembly\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 8.0-9.0; tricarbonyl 0.1-2 mM; total ammonia 0.05-0.2 M; 5 minutes to 6 hours, followed immediately by trapping. Quantify positional imine distributions rather than assuming selectivity.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia is proposed to convert the aldehyde separated from the ketone by CH2 into an imine while leaving the adjacent aldehyde-ketone pair available for later ring construction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative chemoselective application of carbonyl-imine chemistry. The two aldehydes are chemically distinct, but differential hydration does not guarantee selective imination. Reaction at the wrong carbonyl produces incorrect skeletons. This branch is retained to test whether installing the side-chain functionality first improves later ring assembly; it is not an established solution to positional selectivity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate Strecker capture before ring construction\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 8.0-9.0; total cyanide 1-10 mM; minutes to 6 hours. Measure cyanide addition to the remaining carbonyls and determine the lifetime of the desired aminonitrile before warming.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide addition to the selected imine installs the future amino-acid stereocenter and carboxyl-carbon precursor.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The elementary addition is consistent with Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850), but the multifunctional substrate makes this application speculative. Competing cyanohydrin formation and intramolecular condensation of the new amino group with the terminal aldehyde may rapidly divert material into five-membered nitrogen heterocycles. Failure to outpace those losses would exclude the side-chain-first Strecker pathways.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate imidazole assembly on the aminonitrile scaffold\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"313-338 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 8.0-8.8; aminonitrile 0.1-1 mM; formaldehyde 2-20 mM; total ammonia 0.05-0.3 M; 0.5-24 hours. Compare formation rates directly with intramolecular amino-carbonyl cyclization.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The remaining adjacent carbonyls undergo Debus-Radziszewski-type condensation, attaching an imidazole ring to the existing CH2-CH(NH2)-CN side chain.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The ring-construction family is established (Debus, 1858; Radziszewski, 1882), but this substrate-specific conversion is speculative. It avoids carrying an unprotected side-chain aldehyde through ring formation, while introducing a potentially more serious amino-carbonyl cyclization problem. No improved overall selectivity is asserted without measured competition kinetics.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-catalyzed hydration of histidinenitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343-368 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium supplied by acidic volcanic water\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 0.5-1.5; aminonitrile 0.1-10 mM; 1-14 days. Acid demand includes upstream ammonia and carbonate neutralization. Follow nitrile, amide, histidine, and degradation products independently.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile protonation, water addition, and tautomerization generate the primary carboxamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrile hydrolysis is conventional in Strecker amino-acid synthesis (Strecker, 1850), but these are proposed environmental conditions, not a demonstrated histidinenitrile protocol. The important criterion is productive conversion before competing decomposition, not atom balance alone. The required acidity and residence time may be difficult to sustain geochemically.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of histidinamide to histidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-368 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 0.5-1.5; 1-30 days. Determine an integrated time course for amide cleavage and histidine survival. Released ammonia is predominantly ammonium under these conditions.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed addition-elimination at the amide carbonyl releases the amino acid and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The transformation is conventional amide chemistry, but no substrate-specific environmental rate or integrated yield is supplied. It is a potential rate-limiting terminal step. Acceptability requires a demonstrated interval in which histidine accumulation exceeds its loss, together with an acid and dilution budget. Merely assigning a warm surface environment does not establish that interval.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate sulfur-assisted conversion of histidinenitrile to thioamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"313-343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 7.5-9.0; aminonitrile 0.1-5 mM; dissolved sulfide 1-20 mM; 1 hour to 7 days. Determine sulfide remaining after FeS precipitation; total added sulfide is not an adequate availability measure.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrosulfide adds to the nitrile carbon; proton transfers yield the thioamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative substrate-specific extrapolation from nitrile-to-thioamide chemistry. Sulfide offers a chemically relevant anoxic alternative to direct water addition, without invoking an oxidant or photocatalyst. Its benefit is unproven: iron precipitation can remove the reagent, and formation of a thioamide does not establish its productive hydrolysis to histidine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate thioamide hydrolysis through the oxygen-amide channel\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333-368 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 1-3; 1 hour to 14 days. Measure sulfur release and distinguish histidinamide from histidine thioacid. Do not assume that either intermediate accumulates selectively.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water addition to the thiocarbonyl is followed by sulfur elimination, giving the oxygen amide and hydrogen sulfide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative branching assignment within thioamide hydrolysis; no substrate-specific evidence is supplied. This channel and the thioacid channel are competing mechanistic possibilities, not demonstrated independent prebiotic synthesis families. They are separated because the identity and lifetime of the intermediate determine whether sulfur assistance improves recovery.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate thioamide hydrolysis through the thioacid channel\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-363 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 1-3; 1 hour to 14 days. Rapidly sample for the C(=O)SH product and distinguish it from thiocarbonyl tautomers and the oxygen amide.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic addition at the thioamide carbon followed by C-N cleavage releases ammonia and the thioacid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative substrate-specific hydrolysis channel. Protonation can facilitate nitrogen departure, but the competition with sulfur departure is not established for histidinethioamide. This branch should be removed if kinetic and product analysis show that it has negligible flux; it is not counted as experimentally demonstrated merely because its net equation balances.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate hydrolysis of histidine thioacid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-363 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 2-4; 1 hour to 14 days. Quantify thioacid disappearance, histidine recovery, and H2S partitioning between water and gas. Maintain anoxia.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic acyl substitution replaces the sulfur-containing leaving group with hydroxyl.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Chemically plausible thioacid hydrolysis, but the proposed histidine substrate and operating window lack supplied experimental support. H2S loss could favor conversion, but cannot be assumed to occur without simultaneous volatile-reagent losses elsewhere. A measured target yield is required before calling this a milder alternative to amide hydrolysis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate formate-based Ugi capture after imidazole formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-333 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 5.5-7.0; imine-generating carbonyl 0.1-5 mM; formate/formic acid 5-50 mM; methyl isocyanide 1-20 mM; 1 hour to 7 days. Compare dilute water with concentrated wet films; do not assume solvent-free behavior.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Isocyanide addition to the iminium species, formate capture, and acyl rearrangement produce an N-formyl alpha-amino N-methylamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ugi chemistry provides an established alternative means of installing an alpha-amino-acid skeleton (Ugi et al., 1959). This ammonia-derived imine, unprotected imidazole substrate, and aqueous prebiotic application are speculative. Isocyanide hydrolysis and inefficient ammonia-based coupling may dominate. Methyl isocyanide supply is an explicit unclosed boundary condition, not an assumed demonstrated prebiotic inventory.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate formate-based Ugi capture before imidazole formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-323 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 5.5-7.0; selected imine precursor 0.1-2 mM; formate/formic acid 5-50 mM; methyl isocyanide 1-20 mM; 10 minutes to 48 hours. Monitor capture at every carbonyl position.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ugi capture of the isolated-aldehyde-derived imine installs the amino-acid carbon skeleton and simultaneously formylates the amino nitrogen.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative application of the Ugi reaction family (Ugi et al., 1959). Unlike the side-chain-first Strecker branch, the product has an acylated nitrogen, which should reduce its propensity for intramolecular amino-aldehyde cyclization. That is a chemical rationale for testing this order, not evidence of success. Initial imine positional selectivity and survival of the adjacent dicarbonyl remain unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate imidazole assembly on the N-formyl Ugi scaffold\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"313-343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 8.0-8.8; dicarbonyl 0.1-2 mM; formaldehyde 2-20 mM; total ammonia 0.05-0.3 M; 1-72 hours. Track N-formyl retention and carbonyl-derived oligomers.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The remaining adjacent aldehyde-ketone pair condenses with formaldehyde and ammonia to form the imidazole ring.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The reaction family is established (Debus, 1858; Radziszewski, 1882), but this protected amino-acid scaffold is unvalidated. Removing the competing side-chain aldehyde and reducing nitrogen nucleophilicity provide a specific selectivity rationale. Neither consideration supplies a yield estimate, and the stability of the N-formyl group during ring construction must be measured.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate deformylation of the histidine Ugi adduct\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333-353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 1-2; 1-7 days. Quantify cleavage of both amide groups rather than assuming complete selectivity for the formyl group.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of the N-formyl group releases formic acid and exposes the alpha-amino group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Conventional deacylation chemistry motivates this candidate step, but no supplied study establishes the proposed substrate-specific rate. A relatively labile formyl group makes sequential unmasking worth testing. Its removal does not itself yield histidine: the remaining N-methyl carboxamide is a separate, potentially more demanding bottleneck.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate hydrolysis of histidine N-methylamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_025\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-368 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 0-1; 7-90 days. Establish whether acidic volcanic waters can sustain this acidity after neutralization. Measure histidine degradation throughout the same experiment.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed hydrolysis of the secondary carboxamide releases histidine and methylamine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The bond cleavage is ordinary amide hydrolysis, but environmental sufficiency is unproven. A secondary amide may require conditions that are too persistent or acidic for the proposed setting. This is an explicit terminal bottleneck, not a claim that a surface label guarantees recoverable histidine. Failure of its recovery window invalidates both formate-Ugi pathways.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_020\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate acetate-based Ugi capture after imidazole formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_024\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_026\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"303-333 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 5.5-7.0; imine-generating carbonyl 0.1-5 mM; acetate/acetic acid 5-50 mM; methyl isocyanide 1-20 mM; 1-7 days in water or concentrated wet films.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Isocyanide addition, acetate capture, and acyl rearrangement generate the N-acetyl alpha-amino N-methylamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a chemically distinct carboxylic-acid variant of Ugi chemistry (Ugi et al., 1959), not merely a pH variant of the formate reaction. Acetate is a relevant small-molecule feedstock and changes the protective group and subsequent hydrolysis burden. The ammonia-based coupling, methyl isocyanide inventory, and substrate compatibility remain speculative; no superiority to formate is asserted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_021\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate acetate-based Ugi capture before imidazole formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_024\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_027\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-323 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 5.5-7.0; selected imine precursor 0.1-2 mM; acetate/acetic acid 5-50 mM; methyl isocyanide 1-20 mM; 10 minutes to 72 hours. Determine positional product distributions.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ugi capture installs an N-acetyl-protected alpha-amino carboxamide side chain while retaining the adjacent carbonyl pair.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative substrate-specific application of Ugi chemistry (Ugi et al., 1959). Acetyl protection offers a reason to test this sequence: it suppresses the free-amine cyclization liability of the corresponding Strecker intermediate. However, wrong-site carbonyl capture and subsequent deprotection costs can outweigh that benefit. Both must be assessed experimentally.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_022\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate imidazole assembly on the N-acetyl Ugi scaffold\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_027\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_026\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"313-343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 8.0-8.8; dicarbonyl 0.1-2 mM; formaldehyde 2-20 mM; total ammonia 0.05-0.3 M; 1-72 hours. Determine imidazole substitution and retention of both amide groups.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Debus-Radziszewski-type condensation converts the adjacent carbonyl pair into the imidazole ring on the protected amino-acid scaffold.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported only at the reaction-family level (Debus, 1858; Radziszewski, 1882). The absence of a free side-chain aldehyde and free alpha-amino group removes specific competing functionalities, providing chemical merit for testing this candidate. Product identity, yield, and stability under the stated conditions remain unestablished.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_023\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate complete hydrolysis of the N-acetyl histidine Ugi adduct\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_026\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_024\", \"mol_025\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-368 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 0-1; 7-90 days. Follow both amide cleavages and target degradation. This node represents net consecutive hydrolyses without assuming a selective cleavage order or isolable intermediate.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of the N-acetyl group and the N-methyl carboxamide releases histidine, acetic acid, and methylamine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Both bond cleavages are chemically conventional, but their combined completion under plausible surface conditions is not established. This branch can be more demanding than formyl deprotection and is retained as a distinct feedstock-dependent candidate, not a demonstrated improvement. The acetate-Ugi pathways must be rejected if complete hydrolysis cannot outpace histidine loss within a defensible acid and residence-time budget.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Ring-first Strecker synthesis with direct oxygen hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Candidate reference trace: build imidazoleacetaldehyde, capture its imine with cyanide, and hydrolyze through histidinamide. It avoids alcohol oxidation but depends on unvalidated tricarbonyl formation and selective ring assembly.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Side-chain-first Strecker synthesis with direct oxygen hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Install the aminonitrile before forming the imidazole ring. This changes the competing functional groups, but introduces unvalidated imine positional selectivity and rapid intramolecular amino-carbonyl cyclization risks.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Ring-first Strecker synthesis through thioamide and oxygen amide\",\n      \"description\": \"After ring-first aminonitrile formation, test sulfide capture followed by sulfur-eliminating hydrolysis and ordinary amide cleavage. The sulfur-processing advantage and branching fraction are unknown.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Side-chain-first Strecker synthesis through thioamide and oxygen amide\",\n      \"description\": \"Combine side-chain-first assembly with the candidate thioamide-to-oxygen-amide channel. This inherits both the early cyclization risk and the unmeasured sulfur-processing kinetics.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Ring-first Strecker synthesis through thioamide and thioacid\",\n      \"description\": \"Test nitrogen-eliminating thioamide hydrolysis followed by thioacid hydrolysis. This is a competing mechanistic trace, not an independently demonstrated prebiotic synthesis route.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Side-chain-first Strecker synthesis through thioamide and thioacid\",\n      \"description\": \"Combine side-chain-first aminonitrile construction with the candidate thioacid-processing channel. Productive flux through both conditional branches must be established independently.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Ring-first formate-Ugi synthesis\",\n      \"description\": \"Use the heterocyclic imine, formic acid, and methyl isocyanide to install a protected amino-acid amide, then remove both protecting substituents hydrolytically. Isocyanide supply, ammonia-based coupling, and final secondary-amide hydrolysis remain unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_021\", \"mol_023\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Formate-Ugi side-chain installation before ring assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"Install an N-formyl-protected amino-acid side chain before imidazole construction. Protection provides a rationale for reducing free-amine cyclization, but does not resolve the initial carbonyl-site selectivity or final hydrolysis bottleneck.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_021\", \"mol_023\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Ring-first acetate-Ugi synthesis\",\n      \"description\": \"Substitute acetic acid for formic acid in Ugi capture of the heterocyclic imine, giving an N-acetyl product. This is a distinct feedstock and protected-intermediate route, with potentially more demanding complete hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_020\", \"rxn_023\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_026\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetate-Ugi side-chain installation before ring assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"Install an N-acetyl-protected side chain first, build the imidazole ring on the remaining dicarbonyl, and hydrolyze both amides. The sequence is formally coherent but lacks demonstrated positional selectivity, integrated yield, and environmental processing compatibility.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_021\", \"rxn_022\", \"rxn_023\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_027\", \"mol_026\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_013\",\n    \"mol_014\",\n    \"mol_015\",\n    \"mol_017\",\n    \"mol_018\",\n    \"mol_021\",\n    \"mol_026\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_015\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_018\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_026\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_020\", \"rxn_022\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_023\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_019\", \"rxn_023\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Histidine, racemic", "overall_strategy": "This is a connected, explicitly hypothetical synthesis network, not a demonstrated prebiotic preparation. Ten graph-distinct candidate pathways are provided, but they represent combinations of reaction families and competing processing channels, not ten independently established syntheses. A crossed aldol reaction between glyoxal and glycolaldehyde, followed by dehydration, is proposed to supply 2-oxobutanedial. Imidazole formation and installation of the amino-acid precursor are then attempted in either order. Downstream chemistry uses Strecker hydrolysis, sulfur-assisted nitrile processing, or ammonia-based Ugi chemistry. This removes the previous alcohol-photooxidation and hydantoin-cleavage bottlenecks; it does not establish the replacement chemistry. Selective 2-oxobutanedial production, positional selectivity, intermediate survival, and integrated histidine recovery remain unresolved. All numerical conditions below are proposed screening windows, not published substrate-specific operating conditions or measured yields. Historical citations support reaction families only. The network starts with small molecular feedstocks; their simultaneous availability, especially methyl isocyanide, is not established. All stages use locally shaded surface porewaters or pools under anoxic conditions, avoiding an assumed vent-to-surface transfer. Moving between alkaline assembly and acidic hydrolysis stages requires quantified acid inventories, dilution, volatile losses, and organic recovery; purification is not assumed. Abundant Fe2+ can sequester cyanide and sulfide and must be included in experimental material balances. Neutral formulas are bookkeeping representations, not claims about solution speciation. No enantioselection is predicted. The evidence supplied does not justify a high-confidence complete prebiotic synthesis.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "C2H2O2", "iupac_name": "Ethanedial", "common_name": "Glyoxal", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O2/c3-1-2-4/h1-2H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "2-Hydroxyethanal", "common_name": "Glycolaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h2-3H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "Azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "Methanal", "common_name": "Formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-