e", "common_name": null, "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C6H10N4O", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanamide", "common_name": "Histidinamide", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C6H10N4S", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanethioamide", "common_name": "Histidinethioamide", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "C6H9N3OS", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanethioic S-acid", "common_name": "Histidine thioacid, C(=O)SH form", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "C6H9N3O2", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanoic acid", "common_name": "DL-Histidine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C6H9N3O2/c7-4(6(10)11)1-5-2-8-3-9-5/h2-4H,1,7H2,(H,8,9)(H,10,11)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "C8H12N4O2", "iupac_name": "2-Formamido-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)-N-methylpropanamide", "common_name": "N-Formylhistidine N-methylamide", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "C7H10N2O4", "iupac_name": "2-Formamido-N-methyl-4,5-dioxopentanamide", "common_name": null, "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_023", "formula": "C7H12N4O", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)-N-methylpropanamide", "common_name": "Histidine N-methylamide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_024", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "Ethanoic acid", "common_name": "Acetic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c1-2(3)4/h1H3,(H,3,4)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_025", "formula": "CH5N", "iupac_name": "Methanamine", "common_name": "Methylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH5N/c1-2/h2H2,1H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_026", "formula": "C9H14N4O2", "iupac_name": "2-Acetamido-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)-N-methylpropanamide", "common_name": "N-Acetylhistidine N-methylamide", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_027", "formula": "C8H12N2O4", "iupac_name": "2-Acetamido-N-methyl-4,5-dioxopentanamide", "common_name": null, "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Candidate crossed aldol formation of a four-carbon dialdose", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Carbonate-buffered hydroxide"], "conditions": {"temperature": "303-323 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydroxide generated by carbonate alkalinity", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 8.0-9.0; glyoxal 1-10 mM; glycolaldehyde 10-50 mM; 6 hours to 14 days. Use UV-screened, anoxic porewater. Exclude ammonia and cyanide during this stage. These concentrations and residence times are not demonstrated natural inventories."}, "mechanism": "The glycolaldehyde enediolate attacks one glyoxal aldehyde, giving OHC-CH(OH)-CH(OH)-CHO without a redox reaction.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate-specific application of established aldol chemistry. The product has the required four-carbon skeleton and a terminal aldehyde at each end, eliminating the need for a later terminal-alcohol oxidation. Glyoxal hydration, glycolaldehyde self-reaction, disproportionation, and oligomerization may dominate. Detecting trace product is insufficient: quantified selectivity and accumulation in the actual mixture are required. This is an unresolved common entry bottleneck."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Candidate dialdose dehydration to 2-oxobutanedial", "inputs": ["mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "333-363 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium supplied by an acidic volcanic-water input", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 2.5-4.0; dialdose 0.1-10 mM; 1 hour to 7 days. Quantify the acid needed to neutralize upstream carbonate. Use concentrated wet films, not an assumed completely dry reaction."}, "mechanism": "Enolization and elimination of water produce an enol that tautomerizes to OHC-CO-CH2-CHO.", "reasoning": "Speculative deoxyosone-type dehydration. The symmetric dialdose avoids choosing between inequivalent ends, but this does not establish useful yield. Fragmentation, rearrangement, and polymerization can consume both substrate and tricarbonyl product. No supplied evidence establishes an accumulation window. This reaction must be demonstrated before any downstream pathway can be considered complete."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Candidate direct formation of imidazoleacetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "313-343 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 8.0-9.0; tricarbonyl 0.1-1 mM; formaldehyde 2-20 mM; total ammonia 0.05-0.5 M; 1-72 hours. Restricted gas exchange and local UV shielding are required. Measure formaldehyde-ammonia condensation products and all substituted imidazoles."}, "mechanism": "Debus-Radziszewski-type condensation of the adjacent aldehyde-ketone pair with formaldehyde and ammonia forms the imidazole ring while retaining the CH2CHO side chain.", "reasoning": "The imidazole-forming reaction class is established (Debus, 1858; Radziszewski, 1882), but this tricarbonyl substrate and selective retention of its isolated aldehyde are not validated by the supplied evidence. Competing participation of that aldehyde, oligomerization, and formaldehyde sequestration must be measured. Unlike the earlier proposed networks, this step does not require alcohol oxidation afterward; its own selectivity remains a route-blocking uncertainty."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Reversible imine formation from imidazoleacetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 8.5-9.2; aldehyde 0.1-5 mM; total ammonia 0.1-0.5 M; 0.5-24 hours. Treat the imine as a transient equilibrium species, not an isolated purified material."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to the aldehyde; carbinolamine dehydration gives the imine.", "reasoning": "This is the standard carbonyl-to-imine stage of Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850). Its inclusion describes a mechanistic intermediate, not a claim that it accumulates in water. Residual carbonyl compounds compete for ammonia and downstream trapping reagents. Substrate-specific equilibrium and capture rates are needed in the unpurified upstream mixture."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Strecker capture of the heterocyclic imine", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 8.5-9.2; total cyanide 1-20 mM; total ammonia 0.1-0.5 M; 1-48 hours. Determine free cyanide rather than relying on total cyanide. Include Fe2+-cyanide binding and HCN volatilization in the material balance."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer to form racemic histidinenitrile.", "reasoning": "The connectivity follows conventional Strecker synthesis (Strecker, 1850). This does not establish histidinenitrile production in the proposed mixed feed. Cyanohydrin formation is an accompanying equilibrium, not counted here as an independently validated pathway. Useful flux requires cyanide capture to compete successfully with aldehyde loss, iron complexation, and aminonitrile degradation."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Candidate selective imination before imidazole assembly", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 8.0-9.0; tricarbonyl 0.1-2 mM; total ammonia 0.05-0.2 M; 5 minutes to 6 hours, followed immediately by trapping. Quantify positional imine distributions rather than assuming selectivity."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia is proposed to convert the aldehyde separated from the ketone by CH2 into an imine while leaving the adjacent aldehyde-ketone pair available for later ring construction.", "reasoning": "Speculative chemoselective application of carbonyl-imine chemistry. The two aldehydes are chemically distinct, but differential hydration does not guarantee selective imination. Reaction at the wrong carbonyl produces incorrect skeletons. This branch is retained to test whether installing the side-chain functionality first improves later ring assembly; it is not an established solution to positional selectivity."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Candidate Strecker capture before ring construction", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_016"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 8.0-9.0; total cyanide 1-10 mM; minutes to 6 hours. Measure cyanide addition to the remaining carbonyls and determine the lifetime of the desired aminonitrile before warming."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide addition to the selected imine installs the future amino-acid stereocenter and carboxyl-carbon precursor.", "reasoning": "The elementary addition is consistent with Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850), but the multifunctional substrate makes this application speculative. Competing cyanohydrin formation and intramolecular condensation of the new amino group with the terminal aldehyde may rapidly divert material into five-membered nitrogen heterocycles. Failure to outpace those losses would exclude the side-chain-first Strecker pathways."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Candidate imidazole assembly on the aminonitrile scaffold", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_004", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "313-338 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 8.0-8.8; aminonitrile 0.1-1 mM; formaldehyde 2-20 mM; total ammonia 0.05-0.3 M; 0.5-24 hours. Compare formation rates directly with intramolecular amino-carbonyl cyclization."}, "mechanism": "The remaining adjacent carbonyls undergo Debus-Radziszewski-type condensation, attaching an imidazole ring to the existing CH2-CH(NH2)-CN side chain.", "reasoning": "The ring-construction family is established (Debus, 1858; Radziszewski, 1882), but this substrate-specific conversion is speculative. It avoids carrying an unprotected side-chain aldehyde through ring formation, while introducing a potentially more serious amino-carbonyl cyclization problem. No improved overall selectivity is asserted without measured competition kinetics."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Acid-catalyzed hydration of histidinenitrile", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343-368 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium supplied by acidic volcanic water", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 0.5-1.5; aminonitrile 0.1-10 mM; 1-14 days. Acid demand includes upstream ammonia and carbonate neutralization. Follow nitrile, amide, histidine, and degradation products independently."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile protonation, water addition, and tautomerization generate the primary carboxamide.", "reasoning": "Nitrile hydrolysis is conventional in Strecker amino-acid synthesis (Strecker, 1850), but these are proposed environmental conditions, not a demonstrated histidinenitrile protocol. The important criterion is productive conversion before competing decomposition, not atom balance alone. The required acidity and residence time may be difficult to sustain geochemically."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Hydrolysis of histidinamide to histidine", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_020", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-368 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 0.5-1.5; 1-30 days. Determine an integrated time course for amide cleavage and histidine survival. Released ammonia is predominantly ammonium under these conditions."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed addition-elimination at the amide carbonyl releases the amino acid and ammonia.", "reasoning": "The transformation is conventional amide chemistry, but no substrate-specific environmental rate or integrated yield is supplied. It is a potential rate-limiting terminal step. Acceptability requires a demonstrated interval in which histidine accumulation exceeds its loss, together with an acid and dilution budget. Merely assigning a warm surface environment does not establish that interval."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Candidate sulfur-assisted conversion of histidinenitrile to thioamide", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_018"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "313-343 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 7.5-9.0; aminonitrile 0.1-5 mM; dissolved sulfide 1-20 mM; 1 hour to 7 days. Determine sulfide remaining after FeS precipitation; total added sulfide is not an adequate availability measure."}, "mechanism": "Hydrosulfide adds to the nitrile carbon; proton transfers yield the thioamide.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate-specific extrapolation from nitrile-to-thioamide chemistry. Sulfide offers a chemically relevant anoxic alternative to direct water addition, without invoking an oxidant or photocatalyst. Its benefit is unproven: iron precipitation can remove the reagent, and formation of a thioamide does not establish its productive hydrolysis to histidine."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Candidate thioamide hydrolysis through the oxygen-amide channel", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "333-368 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 1-3; 1 hour to 14 days. Measure sulfur release and distinguish histidinamide from histidine thioacid. Do not assume that either intermediate accumulates selectively."}, "mechanism": "Water addition to the thiocarbonyl is followed by sulfur elimination, giving the oxygen amide and hydrogen sulfide.", "reasoning": "Speculative branching assignment within thioamide hydrolysis; no substrate-specific evidence is supplied. This channel and the thioacid channel are competing mechanistic possibilities, not demonstrated independent prebiotic synthesis families. They are separated because the identity and lifetime of the intermediate determine whether sulfur assistance improves recovery."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Candidate thioamide hydrolysis through the thioacid channel", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_019", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-363 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 1-3; 1 hour to 14 days. Rapidly sample for the C(=O)SH product and distinguish it from thiocarbonyl tautomers and the oxygen amide."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic addition at the thioamide carbon followed by C-N cleavage releases ammonia and the thioacid.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate-specific hydrolysis channel. Protonation can facilitate nitrogen departure, but the competition with sulfur departure is not established for histidinethioamide. This branch should be removed if kinetic and product analysis show that it has negligible flux; it is not counted as experimentally demonstrated merely because its net equation balances."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Candidate hydrolysis of histidine thioacid", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_020", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-363 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 2-4; 1 hour to 14 days. Quantify thioacid disappearance, histidine recovery, and H2S partitioning between water and gas. Maintain anoxia."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic acyl substitution replaces the sulfur-containing leaving group with hydroxyl.", "reasoning": "Chemically plausible thioacid hydrolysis, but the proposed histidine substrate and operating window lack supplied experimental support. H2S loss could favor conversion, but cannot be assumed to occur without simultaneous volatile-reagent losses elsewhere. A measured target yield is required before calling this a milder alternative to amide hydrolysis."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Candidate formate-based Ugi capture after imidazole formation", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_008", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_021"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-333 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 5.5-7.0; imine-generating carbonyl 0.1-5 mM; formate/formic acid 5-50 mM; methyl isocyanide 1-20 mM; 1 hour to 7 days. Compare dilute water with concentrated wet films; do not assume solvent-free behavior."}, "mechanism": "Isocyanide addition to the iminium species, formate capture, and acyl rearrangement produce an N-formyl alpha-amino N-methylamide.", "reasoning": "Ugi chemistry provides an established alternative means of installing an alpha-amino-acid skeleton (Ugi et al., 1959). This ammonia-derived imine, unprotected imidazole substrate, and aqueous prebiotic application are speculative. Isocyanide hydrolysis and inefficient ammonia-based coupling may dominate. Methyl isocyanide supply is an explicit unclosed boundary condition, not an assumed demonstrated prebiotic inventory."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Candidate formate-based Ugi capture before imidazole formation", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_008", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_022"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-323 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 5.5-7.0; selected imine precursor 0.1-2 mM; formate/formic acid 5-50 mM; methyl isocyanide 1-20 mM; 10 minutes to 48 hours. Monitor capture at every carbonyl position."}, "mechanism": "Ugi capture of the isolated-aldehyde-derived imine installs the amino-acid carbon skeleton and simultaneously formylates the amino nitrogen.", "reasoning": "Speculative application of the Ugi reaction family (Ugi et al., 1959). Unlike the side-chain-first Strecker branch, the product has an acylated nitrogen, which should reduce its propensity for intramolecular amino-aldehyde cyclization. That is a chemical rationale for testing this order, not evidence of success. Initial imine positional selectivity and survival of the adjacent dicarbonyl remain unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Candidate imidazole assembly on the N-formyl Ugi scaffold", "inputs": ["mol_022", "mol_004", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "313-343 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 8.0-8.8; dicarbonyl 0.1-2 mM; formalde