yl isocyanide 1-20 mM; 10 minutes to 72 hours. Determine positional product distributions."}, "mechanism": "Ugi capture installs an N-acetyl-protected alpha-amino carboxamide side chain while retaining the adjacent carbonyl pair.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate-specific application of Ugi chemistry (Ugi et al., 1959). Acetyl protection offers a reason to test this sequence: it suppresses the free-amine cyclization liability of the corresponding Strecker intermediate. However, wrong-site carbonyl capture and subsequent deprotection costs can outweigh that benefit. Both must be assessed experimentally."}, {"id": "rxn_022", "name": "Candidate imidazole assembly on the N-acetyl Ugi scaffold", "inputs": ["mol_027", "mol_004", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_026", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "313-343 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 8.0-8.8; dicarbonyl 0.1-2 mM; formaldehyde 2-20 mM; total ammonia 0.05-0.3 M; 1-72 hours. Determine imidazole substitution and retention of both amide groups."}, "mechanism": "Debus-Radziszewski-type condensation converts the adjacent carbonyl pair into the imidazole ring on the protected amino-acid scaffold.", "reasoning": "Supported only at the reaction-family level (Debus, 1858; Radziszewski, 1882). The absence of a free side-chain aldehyde and free alpha-amino group removes specific competing functionalities, providing chemical merit for testing this candidate. Product identity, yield, and stability under the stated conditions remain unestablished."}, {"id": "rxn_023", "name": "Candidate complete hydrolysis of the N-acetyl histidine Ugi adduct", "inputs": ["mol_026", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_020", "mol_024", "mol_025"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-368 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 0-1; 7-90 day